[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...exd4 and 5...Bb4) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/97fdd94d-d5ac-4054-8984-6d3e92434bb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...Re8 {Played 50% of the time is the natural ...Re8, bringing the rook to the open file and putting some pressure on e4. We should proceed with Nge2. The simple point is to add some more support to the c3-knight, but also not to block the advance of the f-pawn.} 8.Nge2 8...d6 {Usually, Black proceeds with ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. This is played 58% of the time. Our bishop has done it's job on d2 so we'll move it to g5, pinning the knight. This pin needs to be addressed immediately because Nd5 is a threat.} 9.Bg5 9...h6 {Most logical is ...h6, simply attacking the bishop and forcing a decision. We'll capture Black's knight. We do this because we have a concrete followup.} 10.Bxf6 10...Qxf6 {When Black recaptures... We can jump our knight to d5, hitting Black's queen, bishop and the c7-pawn.} 11.Nd5 11...Qd8 {Black is forced to move the queen all the way back to d8 to defend c7. Now we can capture the bishop.} 12.Nxb4 12...Nxb4 {And when Black recaptures... We can simply move the queen back a square, attacking Black's knight. ...Nxa2+ isn't an issue as it would result in the knight getting trapped.} 13.Qd2 13...a5 {Black supports the knight by advancing their a-pawn. We'll kick Black's knight back.} 14.a3 14...Na6 {Black goes back to a6, looking to route the knight to the c5-square. We'll go f3, supporting the e4-pawn.} 15.f3 15...Nc5 {Black improves their knight. We'll proceed with Nd4, centralizing the knight and opening up our bishop.} 16.Nd4 16...Bd7 {Black's most logical is ...Bd7, simply developing the light-squared bishop.} 17.g4 {And now we'll push g4, beginning the attacking operations on the kingside! The engine might only give a slight edge to White, but this position isn't fun to play as Black. The kingside attack is quickly coming and one inaccurate move can lose the game. Our approach kicked Black's pieces back and ensured we have a strong center, as well. Our plan is to develop the bishop and pawn storm on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...exd4 and 5...Bb4) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/97fdd94d-d5ac-4054-8984-6d3e92434bb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...Ne5 {Black can also get confrontational and attack our queen with ...Ne5. This is the least common of the three options and has been played in only about 7% of games, but it's important to know how to best deal with it. We'll respond with Qb5, double attacking Black's bishop and knight.} 8.Qb5 8...Qe7 {A better way to deal with the double attack is ...Qe7, defending both the bishop and the knight. Against this, we should push a3, simply attacking Black's bishop.} 9.a3 9...Bxc3 {There are no good squares for the bishop, so Black is forced to take our knight and give up the bishop pair. We'll recapture with the bishop.} 10.Bxc3 10...d6 {Black goes ...d6, defending their knight and opening up their bishop.} 11.f3 {And we'll go f3, supporting e4. Black will likely challenge our queen and we'll bring it back to e2. After that, we can look to start a pawn storm!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...exd4 and 5...Bb4) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/97fdd94d-d5ac-4054-8984-6d3e92434bb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...d6 {Black can also play the natural ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. This is played 30% of the time. Since we don't need to play f3, we should use this moment to go Qg3.} 8.Qg3 8...Re8 {Black brings the rook to the open file where it exerts pressure against e4. We'll go f3, supporting the e4-pawn.} 9.f3 9...Be6 {Black proceeds naturally by developing their final minor piece. And now we begin a kingside attack by advancing our h-pawn! We're threatening to advance h5 and h6, so Black needs to do something...} 10.h4 10...d5 {And a pretty logical thing to do is to break in the center with ...d5. This, however, runs into trouble. We'll capture with our e-pawn.} 11.exd5 11...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with their knight. We'll then capture Black's knight.} 12.Nxd5 12...Bxd5 {And when Black recaptures... We can advance with c4, attacking the bishop that's pinned along the d-file! Black is losing a piece here.} 13.c4 13...Bd6 {The only way to try to save a piece is ...Bd6, but this doesn't quite work... We should respond by advancing f4 and blocking the attack on our queen.} 14.f4 14...Be6 {And when Black moves their bishop... We can go Bc3, threatening mate.} 15.Bc3 15...g6 {When Black defends the mate...} 16.c5 {We go c5, winning the d6-bishop! After this forced line and a sharp combination, we are winning. Good game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...exd4 and 5...Bb4) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/97fdd94d-d5ac-4054-8984-6d3e92434bb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...d6 {Black can also play the natural ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. This is played 30% of the time. Since we don't need to play f3, we should use this moment to go Qg3.} 8.Qg3 8...Nh5 {Black can also go ...Nh5, attacking our queen and forcing it to move away from the active g3-square. Against this, we'll go Qe3, and the plan here is pretty standard. We want to push on the kingside.} 9.Qe3 9...Bc5 {And if Black brings their bishop back and attacks our queen once more...} 10.Qe1 {We can simply bring the queen back. Our plan is unaltered - pawn storm!} *

