1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...Re8 {Played 50% of the time is the natural ...Re8, bringing the rook to the open file and putting some pressure on e4. We should proceed with Nge2. The simple point is to add some more support to the c3-knight, but also not to block the advance of the f-pawn.} 8.Nge2 8...d6 {Usually, Black proceeds with ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. This is played 58% of the time. Our bishop has done it's job on d2 so we'll move it to g5, pinning the knight. This pin needs to be addressed immediately because Nd5 is a threat.} 9.Bg5 9...Ne5 {Black can also go ...Ne5, attacking our queen, but this is quite bad. We'll simply move our queen to g3. We are now threatening two things: Nd5, and f4, followed by e5.} 10.Qg3 10...Ng6 {Black can try to move out of the way by bringing the knight back to g6...} 11.h4 {But now we advance the h-pawn. White has a decisive attack on the kingside. With h5 and Nd5 on the way, Black's position is bound to collapse.} *

