[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...exd4 and 5...Bb4) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/97fdd94d-d5ac-4054-8984-6d3e92434bb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...Re8 {Played 50% of the time is the natural ...Re8, bringing the rook to the open file and putting some pressure on e4. We should proceed with Nge2. The simple point is to add some more support to the c3-knight, but also not to block the advance of the f-pawn.} 8.Nge2 8...Ne5 {Black might also try to get aggressive with ...Ne5, attacking our queen. This is played 27% of the time. Against this, we'll go for Qg3. If Black plays slowly now, we'll go Bg5 and look to attack on the kingside...} 9.Qg3 9...Nh5 {That's why Black really should attack the queen once again with ...Nh5. And we should move our queen to e3. If Black now decides to stop being confrontational, we'll look to proceed with Ng3 and f4 at the right moment.} 10.Qe3 10...Nc4 {And if Black insists on confrontation by attacking our queen again... We should respond with Qd4.} 11.Qd4 11...Nxd2 {And when Black takes our bishop... We'll take Black's bishop.} 12.Qxb4 12...Nxf1 {The knight has no way out, so Black is forced to capture the f1-bishop.} 13.Rhxf1 {And we'll recapture with the h-rook. White is better. We have more space and our pieces are more active. The plan here is simple - pawnstorm!} *

