1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This essentially means that we should try to attack Black on the kingside, and Black should try to attack us on the queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...Ne5 {Black can also get confrontational and attack our queen with ...Ne5. This is the least common of the three options and has been played in only about 7% of games, but it's important to know how to best deal with it. We'll respond with Qb5, double attacking Black's bishop and knight.} 8.Qb5 8...Bxc3 {The most common and natural response is to simply take on c3. This, however, isn't good. Not only is Black giving us the bishop pair, but also improving the placement of our dark-squared bishop. We should recapture with our bishop. If Black now opts to retreat the knight to either c6 or g6, we'll proceed with f3, supporting e4, and then follow it up with the typical pawn storm.} 9.Bxc3 9...Neg4 {And if Black keeps trying to pose problems by threatening ...Nxf2...} 10.Qe2 {We can simply bring our queen back and defend against Black's threat. White is much better as Black has made a lot of superficial threats and has only managed to improve our position. We'll go f3 to kick back the knight and support the e4-pawn, and follow up with the pawn storm on the kingside.} *

