1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6, which is played roughly a third of the time. We strike in the center with d4! This unconventional pawn advance is the essence of the Dark Vienna. The idea is to open up the center and get dynamic middlegame positions filled with tactics and attacking ideas.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular and natural response for Black, which is capturing on d4, and it's played 65% of the time. Black simply wants to force our queen out and use it as a target. We should, of course, recapture. Our queen can be targeted here, but that doesn't bother us all that much.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square. We did waste a bit of time with our queen, but the center has opened up and we have an active plan: develop the dark-squared bishop, castle queenside and develop an attack.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb4 {By far the most popular continuation is ...Bb4, developing the dark-squared bishop and pinning our knight. This move is played half the time. We'll respond by developing our dark-squared bishop and breaking the pin.} 6.Bd2 6...Bxc3 {Played 10% of the time is ...Bxc3. This really isn't great. Black is giving up the bishop pair unprovoked and improving our dark-squared bishop. We'll recapture with our bishop.} 7.Bxc3 7...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside, which is more natural in this case.} 8.O-O-O 8...Re8 {Black then goes ...Re8, bringing the rook to the open file and attacking e4. We'll go f3, supporting the e4-pawn.} 9.f3 9...d6 {Black proceeds naturally with ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll develop our knight to e2, with the idea to bring it to the f4-square.} 10.Ne2 10...Be6 {Black proceeds by developing their final minor piece to an active square. I suggest we proceed with a3, making sure there's never any ...Bxa2 ideas.} 11.a3 11...a6 {Black goes ...a6, controlling b5 and supporting a future b-pawn advance. We'll continue by improving the placement of our knight.} 12.Nf4 12...Ne5 {Black might then centralize their knight and attack our queen.} 13.Qe3 {But we can simply respond by moving our queen over one square. White is much better here. We have more space and better pieces. The immediate threat is Bxe5 to win a piece, and our plan in this position is to develop our light-squared bishop and follow up with a pawn storm.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...exd4 and 5...Bc5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/1a1dfd56-fa38-4a6d-9e91-db2aba1f4425/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square.} 5.Qd3 5...Bc5 {Black's second option is to develop the dark-squared bishop to c5. This is played 15% of the time and the idea is to quickly target the f2-pawn. Against this, we'll develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...d6 {Black can also proceed with ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll respond by castling queenside. Once again, we're gambiting a pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...Bxf2 {Black captures the f2-pawn. We proceed by developing our knight. Development is more essential than material here.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Black castles their king. And now it's important that we go Qe2, attacking Black's bishop.} 9.Qe2 9...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to safety. We go Bg5, pinning the knight and looking to follow up with Nd5. Allowing Nd5 is not a good idea for Black, so they need to deal with the pin immediately...} 10.Bg5 10...h6 {The way to do so is ...h6, attacking the bishop and forcing a decision. We'll slide the bishop back a square, maintaining the pin and posing Black a serious question: Can they go ...g5?} 11.Bh4 11...g5 {Black decides to advance ...g5. Now we sacrifice the knight!} 12.Nxg5 12...hxg5 {Black recaptures.} 13.Bxg5 {And we capture the g5-pawn with our bishop, renewing the powerful pin. Black is in serious trouble here. Nd5 is coming, the king is wide open and our queen and rook can quickly join the attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...exd4 and 5...Bc5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/1a1dfd56-fa38-4a6d-9e91-db2aba1f4425/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square.} 5.Qd3 5...Bc5 {Black's second option is to develop the dark-squared bishop to c5. This is played 15% of the time and the idea is to quickly target the f2-pawn. Against this, we'll develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...d6 {Black can also proceed with ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll respond by castling queenside. Once again, we're gambiting a pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...Bxf2 {Black captures the f2-pawn. We proceed by developing our knight. Development is more essential than material here.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Black castles their king. And now it's important that we go Qe2, attacking Black's bishop.} 9.Qe2 9...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to safety. We go Bg5, pinning the knight and looking to follow up with Nd5. Allowing Nd5 is not a good idea for Black, so they need to deal with the pin immediately...} 10.Bg5 10...h6 {The way to do so is ...h6, attacking the bishop and forcing a decision. We'll slide the bishop back a square, maintaining the pin and posing Black a serious question: Can they go ...g5?} 11.Bh4 11...Re8 {Black can also refuse to break the pin immediately and go ...Re8 instead. Now we'll go Nd5, putting some pressure on the pinned f6-knight.} 12.Nd5 12...g5 {Black must now go ...g5 in order to break the pin on their knight. We'll respond by sacrificing the knight. Capturing the knight isn't a good idea (as we've seen in the line where Black advances ...g5 immediately)...} 13.Nxg5 13...Nxd5 {So Black captures the d5-knight.} 14.Rxd5 {And we recapture with the rook. The engine finds a way to hold equality here (with ...Qf6), but the position is far from easy to play for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...exd4 and 5...Bc5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/1a1dfd56-fa38-4a6d-9e91-db2aba1f4425/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square.} 5.Qd3 5...Bc5 {Black's second option is to develop the dark-squared bishop to c5. This is played 15% of the time and the idea is to quickly target the f2-pawn. Against this, we'll develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This is where things get spicy as we are sacrificing the f2-pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...d6 {Black can also proceed in a more calm manner with ...d6, simply opening up the light-squared bishop. We can now go f3, supporting e4 and preparing a future g-pawn advance.} 8.f3 8...Be6 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to the most active square. Now I suggest we go Qd2 in order to sidestep any ...Nb4 or ...Ne5 ideas.} 9.Qd2 9...a6 {A logical way to proceed is ...a6, preparing to advance the b-pawn. We'll improve our king's placement.} 10.Kb1 10...b5 {Black follows up with ...b5, launching an attack on the queenside. We'll respond with h4, launching our own attack on the opposite side.} 11.h4 11...b4 {Black advances the b-pawn once more and attacks our knight. We should respond Na4, eyeing Black's dark-squared bishop.} 12.Na4 12...Ba7 {Black doesn't want to lose their strong bishop, so they retreat it. We keep advancing our h-pawn.} 13.h5 13...h6 {And when Black stops our h-pawn...} 14.g4 {Here comes the reinforcement! We are threatening to rip apart the kingside with g5 and Black has to defend very accurately. Practically speaking, White definitely has an edge with the kingside attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...exd4 and 5...Bc5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/1a1dfd56-fa38-4a6d-9e91-db2aba1f4425/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square.} 5.Qd3 5...Bc5 {Black's second option is to develop the dark-squared bishop to c5. This is played 15% of the time and the idea is to quickly target the f2-pawn. Against this, we'll develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This is where things get spicy as we are sacrificing the f2-pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...Bxf2 {Black decides to call our bluff and captures the free pawn on f2. Now we'll advance our e-pawn and attack Black's knight, forcing it to move to an awkward square. If Black now opts for ...Ne8 or ...Ng4, we can proceed with Nf3 and more than sufficient compensation for the pawn.} 8.e5 8...Nh5 {And if Black chooses to go ...Nh5, attacking our dark-squared bishop... We can simply slide the bishop back. This position is already quite difficult for Black. We have compensation for the pawn and Qf3 is a serious threat.} 9.Bd2 9...Nxe5 {If Black now gets greedy...} 10.Qf5 {We win material with Qf5, triple attacking Black's minor pieces! This is just a sample of how easily you can be winning if Black doesn't respect your Dark Vienna setup!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...exd4 and 5...Bc5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/1a1dfd56-fa38-4a6d-9e91-db2aba1f4425/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. Instead of proceeding in the most standard way with 2. Nf3, we will develop the queenside knight to c3. This move characterizes the Vienna.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nf6. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. And we'll respond by sliding our queen back one square.} 5.Qd3 5...Bc5 {Black's second option is to develop the dark-squared bishop to c5. This is played 15% of the time and the idea is to quickly target the f2-pawn. Against this, we'll develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We'll respond by castling queenside. This is where things get spicy as we are sacrificing the f2-pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...Ng4 {Black can also go ...Ng4, looking to capture the f2-pawn with the knight. We have to stop ...Nxf2 from coming with a fork, so we play Nh3, adding a defender to the f2-pawn.} 8.Nh3 8...Nxf2 {Black captures f2 with their knight. We capture Black's knight.} 9.Nxf2 9...Bxf2 {Black recaptures. And now we can advance e5, taking some space and opening up our queen's vision towards the kingside.} 10.e5 10...Re8 {If Black now attacks the e5-pawn... We have the strong response Ne4, attacking Black's bishop on f2.} 11.Ne4 11...Bb6 {And when Black saves their bishop... We go Ng5, threatening Qxh7+!} 12.Ng5 12...g6 {Black has to defend with ...g6. Now we swing our queen over to the h-file, threatening Qxh7+ again.} 13.Qh3 13...h5 {The only way to defend against the threat of Qxh7+ is to advance ...h5.} 14.Qg3 {And now we go Qg3, supporting the e5-pawn and sidestepping any discoveries from Black's c8-bishop. Black is simply defenseless here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...exd4 (other lines) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/de339a38-0cf4-4845-a848-62b1b0848358/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {2...Nf6 is standard development in e4-e5 openings. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. We'll slide back to d3.} 5.Qd3 5...d6 {Third in popularity is ...d6, simply opening up the light-squared bishop. This is played about 13% of the time. Against ...d6, we will develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...Be7 {Black's most natural way to proceed is ...Be7, preparing ...O-O. We'll castle queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...O-O {Black castles kingside. Now we go f3, supporting e4 and preparing a future g-pawn advance.} 8.f3 8...Be6 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to the most active square. We'll improve our king's placement.} 9.Kb1 9...a6 {A logical way to proceed is ...a6, preparing to advance the b-pawn. But ...b5 doesn't worry us, as we can proceed by developing the g1-knight.} 10.Nge2 10...b5 {And when Black advances ...b5, looking to attack on the queenside... We'll respond with g4, attacking on the opposite side of the board!} 11.g4 11...b4 {If Black keeps advancing on the queenside and attacks our knight...} 12.Nd5 {We can simply respond with Nd5. This is an excellent position for White. We have more space, better pieces and a more potent attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...exd4 (other lines) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/de339a38-0cf4-4845-a848-62b1b0848358/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {2...Nf6 is standard development in e4-e5 openings. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. We'll slide back to d3.} 5.Qd3 5...d6 {Third in popularity is ...d6, simply opening up the light-squared bishop. This is played about 13% of the time. Against ...d6, we will develop our dark-squared bishop to f4 and prepare queenside castling.} 6.Bf4 6...Be7 {Black's most natural way to proceed is ...Be7, preparing ...O-O. We'll castle queenside.} 7.O-O-O 7...O-O {Black castles kingside. Now we go f3, supporting e4 and preparing a future g-pawn advance.} 8.f3 8...Be6 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to the most active square. We'll improve our king's placement.} 9.Kb1 9...Nb4 {Black could go ...Nb4, attacking our queen, but this shouldn't worry us.} 10.Qd2 {We can simply slide the queen back one square and kick Black's knight back with a3 on the next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...exd4 (other lines) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/de339a38-0cf4-4845-a848-62b1b0848358/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We'll develop the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {2...Nf6 is standard development in e4-e5 openings. We strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. We should, of course, recapture.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black proceeds by developing their knight and attacking our queen. We'll slide back to d3.} 5.Qd3 5...Nb4 {Black's fourth and final option is the confrontational ...Nb4, attacking our queen, eyeing the c2-pawn and looking to advance the d-pawn. This is played roughly 7% of the time. The best response is Qe2. We save our queen, guard c2 and stop ...d5.} 6.Qe2 6...d6 {Black's most logical way to continue is ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop and covering the e5-square. Now we develop our dark-squared bishop to f4, preparing O-O-O.} 7.Bf4 7...Bg4 {More often than not, Black plays ...Bg4, developing the light-squared bishop and attacking our queen. We should respond with f3, blocking the attack on our queen, attacking the bishop and supporting the e4-pawn.} 8.f3 8...Be6 {Black brings the bishop back to the most active square. We go a3, kicking Black's knight away.} 9.a3 9...Nc6 {Black retreats their knight. Now we simply castle queenside.} 10.O-O-O 10...Be7 {And when Black goes for the natural ...Be7, preparing to castle kingside... We'll advance our h-pawn! The plan is simple - attack Black's kingside by advancing the pawns.} 11.h4 11...O-O {Black proceeds by castling kingside. We keep advancing our h-pawn.} 12.h5 12...h6 {And when Black stops the h-pawn...} 13.g4 {We can advance our g-pawn, looking to rip apart the kingside with g5! White is better with a strong attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...d6 {Black's third option is ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. This move is played about 8% of the time and, unfortunately, isn't all that dumb even though it enables us to take away Black's castling rights. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...dxe5 {Black recaptures. Now we trade the queens, forcing Black to recapture with their king.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {And Black recaptures, losing castling rights but arguing that it's not a big deal without the queens on the board. We proceed by developing our light-squared bishop to c4, attacking f7.} 6.Bc4 6...Ke8 {27% of the time Black chooses to defend the f7-pawn with their king. This is quite natural, but allows us to quickly develop with a strong initiative. Against ...Ke8, we should develop our knight to f3 and attack the e5-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...Nc6 {Black goes ...Nc6, defending e5. Now we go Ng5, attacking f7.} 8.Ng5 8...Nd8 {Black goes ...Nd8, defending f7. We gallop our other knight forward, attacking the undefended c7-pawn.} 9.Nb5 9...Bd6 {Black goes ...Bd6, defending c7. Now we capture Black's bishop. Trading here is beneficial for us.} 10.Nxd6+ 10...cxd6 {And when Black recaptures... We strike at the center with f4!} 11.f4 11...h6 {Black naturally proceeds with ...h6, trying to kick away our active knight. We'll respond by taking on e5 and counterattacking Black's f6-knight.} 12.fxe5 12...dxe5 {Black recaptures. Our knight was attacked, so we bring it back to f3, attacking the e5-pawn.} 13.Nf3 13...Nc6 {Black goes ...Nc6, defending e5. We continue by castling kingside.} 14.O-O 14...Nxe4 {And if Black captures the e4-pawn... We can respond with Bd5, attacking the e4-knight and looking to remove the defender of the e5-pawn.} 15.Bd5 15...Nd6 {Black brings their knight to safety. We take Black's knight, eliminating the only defender of the e5-pawn.} 16.Bxc6+ 16...bxc6 {Black recaptures.} 17.Nxe5 {And we simply capture the e5-pawn. White has a very pleasant advantage. Our pieces are more active, Black's c6 and f7-pawns are hard to defend, and Black's king is stuck in the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...d6 {Black's third option is ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. This move is played about 8% of the time and, unfortunately, isn't all that dumb even though it enables us to take away Black's castling rights. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...dxe5 {Black recaptures. Now we trade the queens, forcing Black to recapture with their king.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {And Black recaptures, losing castling rights but arguing that it's not a big deal without the queens on the board. We proceed by developing our light-squared bishop to c4, attacking f7.} 6.Bc4 6...Ke8 {27% of the time Black chooses to defend the f7-pawn with their king. This is quite natural, but allows us to quickly develop with a strong initiative. Against ...Ke8, we should develop our knight to f3 and attack the e5-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...Nc6 {Black goes ...Nc6, defending e5. Now we go Ng5, attacking f7.} 8.Ng5 8...Nd8 {Black goes ...Nd8, defending f7. We gallop our other knight forward, attacking the undefended c7-pawn.} 9.Nb5 9...Bb4+ {Black can also play ...Bb4+, with the idea to defend c7 from the a5-square. We'll respond by blocking the check and offering a trade of bishops.} 10.Bd2 10...Bxd2+ {Black trades the bishops. We recapture, still threatening Nxc7+.} 11.Kxd2 11...Kd7 {Black has to defend with ...Kd7.} 12.Rad1 {And we bring a rook to the open file. Our position is very comfortable and we'll look to proceed with Kc1+.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...Nc6 {The second most common way to proceed without taking d4 is ...Nc6. This really isn't good for Black and is played roughly 8% of the time. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...Nxe5 {And when Black recaptures... We can advance our f-pawn, taking space and kicking the knight away. Black now has to retreat their knight and there are two options...} 5.f4 5...Nc6 {The more common retreat is ...Nc6. This is played about 58% of the time. Now we advance e5, taking more space and attacking Black's knight.} 6.e5 6...Ng8 {The only safe square for Black's knight is its home square on g8. We proceed by developing our knight.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Black goes ...d6, striking at our center and opening the light-squared bishop. Now we develop our light-squared bishop, pinning Black's knight.} 8.Bb5 8...Bd7 {Black goes ...Bd7, breaking the pin. I suggest we proceed with Qe2, lining up our queen with the black king and threatening to capture the d6-pawn.} 9.Qe2 9...dxe5 {Seeing that we were threatening to capture, Black releases the tension.} 10.fxe5 {And we recapture with the f-pawn. Our position is fantastic and the engine gives a winning advantage. We'll look to proceed with Bg5 and castling queenside or kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...Nc6 {The second most common way to proceed without taking d4 is ...Nc6. This really isn't good for Black and is played roughly 8% of the time. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...Nxe5 {And when Black recaptures... We can advance our f-pawn, taking space and kicking the knight away. Black now has to retreat their knight and there are two options...} 5.f4 5...Ng6 {The less common retreat is ...Ng6. This is played about 35% of the time. We should advance e5, taking more space and attacking Black's knight.} 6.e5 6...Ng8 {The only safe square for Black's knight is its home square on g8. We proceed by developing our knight.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Black goes ...d6, striking at our center and opening the light-squared bishop. Now, since there's no knight on c6 that we can pin, we'll develop our light-squared bishop to c4, eyeing f7.} 8.Bc4 8...Bg4 {The most common and natural way to proceed is ...Bg4, pinning our knight. This, however, leaves the b7-pawn undefended... And we have the very powerful Qd5, threatening mate and attacking b7.} 9.Qd5 9...Be6 {Black stops checkmate with ...Be6. But now we can simply capture b7.} 10.Qxb7 10...Bxc4 {And when Black takes our bishop... We have Qc6+, winning our piece back immediately via a fork.} 11.Qc6+ 11...Ke7 {...Qd7 would drop the a8-rook, so Black is forced to move their king.} 12.Qxc4 {And we simply capture the bishop. White is completely winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...d6 {Black's third option is ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. This move is played about 8% of the time and, unfortunately, isn't all that dumb even though it enables us to take away Black's castling rights. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...dxe5 {Black recaptures. Now we trade the queens, forcing Black to recapture with their king.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {And Black recaptures, losing castling rights but arguing that it's not a big deal without the queens on the board. We proceed by developing our light-squared bishop to c4, attacking f7.} 6.Bc4 6...Be6 {The most common way to defend f7 is to offer a bishop trade with ...Be6. This is played 52% of the time. We'll trade the bishops, giving Black doubled isolated pawns on the e-file.} 7.Bxe6 7...fxe6 {Black recaptures. Now I suggest we proceed with f3, adding some support to the e4-pawn.} 8.f3 8...Bd6 {Black goes ...Bd6, developing their bishop and supporting the e5-pawn. We develop our g1-knight to e2.} 9.Nge2 9...Ke7 {Black improves their king and adds some support to the e6-pawn.} 10.Be3 {And we develop our final minor piece. White is definitely slightly better in this queenless middlegame thanks to Black's doubled and isolated pawns. Regarding some plans from here on, the engine really likes to advance h4 and set up the knights on d2 and d3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...d6 {Black's third option is ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. This move is played about 8% of the time and, unfortunately, isn't all that dumb even though it enables us to take away Black's castling rights. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...dxe5 {Black recaptures. Now we trade the queens, forcing Black to recapture with their king.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {And Black recaptures, losing castling rights but arguing that it's not a big deal without the queens on the board. We proceed by developing our light-squared bishop to c4, attacking f7.} 6.Bc4 6...Bb4 {Black can also go ...Bb4, dealing with the threat by pinning our knight and creating a counter-threat of ...Nxe4. This is played about 8% of the time. We should break the pin immediately.} 7.Bd2 7...Bxc3 {Black captures the c3-knight. This move leads to a pretty forcing sequence of moves... We recapture with our bishop.} 8.Bxc3 8...Nxe4 {Black then captures the e4-pawn. We respond by taking the e5-pawn.} 9.Bxe5 9...Re8 {A natural way to proceed is ...Re8, attacking our bishop and threatening some discoveries if it moves away. We castle queenside with check.} 10.O-O-O+ 10...Bd7 {Black covers up with their bishop. Now we go Bg3, moving the bishop out of harm's way and defending f2.} 11.Bg3 11...Nxg3 {And if Black takes our bishop...} 12.hxg3 {We can simply recapture with the h-pawn, opening the file for our rook. White has a winning advantage. Black simply can't defend both f7 and h7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nf6 / Study 4: Black Doesn't Capture d4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/95d3c641-b690-4fb5-8e47-731f1e30ab5c/studies/46fb9146-4a1b-4bc2-9586-a13bb3149cf4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nf6. And now we play d4, the signature move of the Dark Vienna! This study is about what to do if Black doesn't capture the d4-pawn.} 3.d4 3...Bb4 {The most common way to proceed without taking the d4-pawn is ...Bb4. Black develops their bishop, pins our knight and threatens to capture e4. We'll proceed by capturing on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...Nxe4 {And when Black captures e4... We have the powerful Qg4, exploiting Black's undefended pawn and pieces. ...Nf6 doesn't make sense in these positions for Black. After 5.Qg4 we are totally winning.} 5.Qg4 5...d5 {To illustrate, suppose 5...d5, attacking the queen. 5...Nxc3 would allow 6.Qxb4. This is not only a free pawn, but Black's kingside is completely wrecked.} 6.Qxg7 6...Rf8 {We are winning on the kingside. White should force the bishop to take on c3.} 7.a3 7...Bxc3+ {After they take, we don't mind losing a queenside pawn.} 8.bxc3 {We can simply recapture. If Nxc3, we have Bh6 to pick up the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/a07ef1e9-4bba-478e-8d99-2f0cfe635bc3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...Be6 {Black's second option is to develop their light-squared bishop to e6. This is played 27% of the time. We'll respond by striking in the center.} 9.d4 9...exd4 {Black captures the d4-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight.} 10.Nxd4 10...Nxd4 {Black decides to trade the knights. And we recapture with our queen. Black has two options in this position but the more common one, castling, directly transposes to the main line.} 11.Qxd4 11...Qd7 {Black's other option is ...Qd7. The point is to follow up with ...Bh3 and trade the light-squared bishops. Against this, we'll go Bg5, putting some pressure on Black's knight.} 12.Bg5 12...Ra4 {Best is ...Ra4, attacking our queen. This is unlikely to occur as ...Ra4 isn't an easy move to spot, but we should take a look at how to deal with it. We'll move our queen to c3, still keeping eyes on Black's knight. ...Nxe4 now fails to Qxg7.} 13.Qc3 13...Rc4 {Black attacks our queen again. This forces our queen to move to a square from which it can no longer put pressure on the f6-knight.} 14.Qd3 {And we'll go Qd3, protecting e4. Black has avoided the dangers, but White is still slightly better here. We can look to proceed by capturing Black's knight, followed by b3 and c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/a07ef1e9-4bba-478e-8d99-2f0cfe635bc3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Usually, Black also castles. And now we advance our d-pawn! Our idea here is to open the center and follow up by fianchettoing our dark-squared bishop. Black has two ways to respond.} 9.d4 9...exd4 {The most common and natural response is to take the d4-pawn. We'll simply recapture with our knight.} 10.Nxd4 10...Nxd4 {Most often, Black trades the knights. This happens 40% of the time. We'll recapture with our queen.} 11.Qxd4 11...Be6 {Black proceeds by developing their bishop to the most active square. We'll go b3, preparing to fianchetto.} 12.b3 12...Qd7 {Logical is ...Qd7, looking to go ...Bh3 and connecting the rooks. And we should respond with Re1. This move is important because it gets the rook off of the f1-square.} 13.Re1 13...Bh3 {So when Black goes to trade off the light-squared bishops...} 14.Bh1 {We can slide the bishop back. Our position is great. We've got the pair of bishops and more space. The plan from here is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, push a4, and bring the a-rook to the d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/a07ef1e9-4bba-478e-8d99-2f0cfe635bc3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Usually, Black also castles. And now we advance our d-pawn! Our idea here is to open the center and follow up by fianchettoing our dark-squared bishop. Black has two ways to respond.} 9.d4 9...exd4 {The most common and natural response is to take the d4-pawn. We'll simply recapture with our knight.} 10.Nxd4 10...Nxd4 {Most often, Black trades the knights. This happens 40% of the time. We'll recapture with our queen.} 11.Qxd4 11...Re8 {Black can also proceed with ...Re8. We'll continue with our plan of fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 12.b3 12...c5 {Black might then go ...c5, taking space and attacking our queen. We'll slide the queen back a square.} 13.Qd3 13...Be6 {Black's most logical continuation is to develop the light-squared bishop.} 14.Bb2 {And we'll fianchetto our bishop. White is doing great here. We've got the bishop pair and Black has some serious weaknesses to worry about. We can look to advance with c4 and put pressure on the weak d6-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/a07ef1e9-4bba-478e-8d99-2f0cfe635bc3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb4 {Black can also respond with ...Bb4. This isn't all that great for Black because it allows us to target the bishop and gain a lot of space. We'll go a3, attacking the bishop.} 7.a3 7...Ba5 {And when Black moves the bishop... We'll advance our b-pawn, attacking the bishop again and taking space.} 8.b4 8...Bb6 {Black is forced to go ...Bb6. And now we'll capture the bishop.} 9.Nxb6 9...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 10.Bb2 10...O-O {Black's most logical move is to castle. We'll also proceed by castling. If Black now plays a slow move, we'll follow up by advancing our d-pawn.} 11.O-O 11...Bg4 {That's why Black usually goes ...Bg4, pinning the knight and preventing d4. We'll respond by immediately challenging Black's bishop. In this case, I prefer h3 to f3. We're going for a slightly different plan.} 12.h3 12...Bh5 {Usually, Black responds with ...Bh5. We go g4, attacking the bishop again.} 13.g4 13...Bg6 {Black goes ...Bg6 and attacks e4.} 14.d3 {And we play d3, supporting e4. Our plan here is to push f4. The threat of f5 will likely force Black to take and open up our dark-squared bishop. White has a pleasant edge and great attacking prospects on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 11"]
