1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb4 {Black can also respond with ...Bb4. This isn't all that great for Black because it allows us to target the bishop and gain a lot of space. We'll go a3, attacking the bishop.} 7.a3 7...Ba5 {And when Black moves the bishop... We'll advance our b-pawn, attacking the bishop again and taking space.} 8.b4 8...Bb6 {Black is forced to go ...Bb6. And now we'll capture the bishop.} 9.Nxb6 9...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 10.Bb2 10...O-O {Black's most logical move is to castle. We'll also proceed by castling. If Black now plays a slow move, we'll follow up by advancing our d-pawn.} 11.O-O 11...Bg4 {That's why Black usually goes ...Bg4, pinning the knight and preventing d4. We'll respond by immediately challenging Black's bishop. In this case, I prefer h3 to f3. We're going for a slightly different plan.} 12.h3 12...Bxe2 {Black can also capture the knight. The idea is to follow up with ...Nd4. We'll recapture with our queen.} 13.Qxe2 13...Nd4 {Black will then proceed with ...Nd4. We'll respond by taking the knight.} 14.Bxd4 14...exd4 {And when Black recaptures... We'll go Qc4, attacking the d4-pawn.} 15.Qc4 15...c5 {The only way to defend d4 is ...c5.} 16.Rfb1 {And now we bring the f-rook to b1. The play has completely shifted to the queenside and we're more equipped to fight on that side. Our plan is to open up the b-file by taking on c5. From there, we'll look to exert pressure towards the b-pawns.} *

