1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Usually, Black also castles. And now we advance our d-pawn! Our idea here is to open the center and follow up by fianchettoing our dark-squared bishop. Black has two ways to respond.} 9.d4 9...exd4 {The most common and natural response is to take the d4-pawn. We'll simply recapture with our knight.} 10.Nxd4 10...Re8 {Black's other option is to bring the rook to the e-file and attack e4. This is played 30% of the time. We'll respond by taking Black's knight.} 11.Nxc6 11...bxc6 {Black recaptures. Now we go Re1, defending e4.} 12.Re1 12...Bb7 {Black develops their bishop to b7. The idea is to advance with ...c5 and put even more pressure on the e4-pawn. We'll continue with our plan of fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 13.b3 13...c5 {Black goes ...c5, opening up their bishop and attacking the e4-pawn. Looks like we have to defend e4... But we can simply fianchetto! This move defends e4 tactically.} 14.Bb2 14...Nxe4 {If Black were to take the e4-pawn...} 15.Qg4 {We've got Qg4, double attacking Black's knight and threatening mate on g7. White is completely winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 4"]
