1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Usually, Black also castles. And now we advance our d-pawn! Our idea here is to open the center and follow up by fianchettoing our dark-squared bishop. Black has two ways to respond.} 9.d4 9...Bg4 {Black's other option in this position is ...Bg4, pinning our knight. This is played 15% of the time. We'll go f3, attacking the bishop.} 10.f3 10...Bh5 {The first of two retreats is ...Bh5. We go g4, attacking the bishop again.} 11.g4 11...Bg6 {Black has to save the bishop. Now we advance our d-pawn, taking space and attacking Black's knight.} 12.d5 12...Ne7 {Black moves their knight back.} 13.c4 {And we can advance our c-pawn. White is better. We've got more space on all three parts of the board. We'll develop the dark-squared bishop to e3, bring our knight to g3 and start advancing our h-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/a07ef1e9-4bba-478e-8d99-2f0cfe635bc3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Usually, Black also castles. And now we advance our d-pawn! Our idea here is to open the center and follow up by fianchettoing our dark-squared bishop. Black has two ways to respond.} 9.d4 9...Bg4 {Black's other option in this position is ...Bg4, pinning our knight. This is played 15% of the time. We'll go f3, attacking the bishop.} 10.f3 10...Bd7 {Black's other retreat is ...Bd7. We'll go b3, preparing to fianchetto.} 11.b3 11...Qc8 {Logical is ...Qc8, looking to go ...Bh3. But now we change our plan and go Bg5, exploiting the fact that Black's queen has left the d8-square and threatening to ruin Black's structure.} 12.Bg5 12...Bh3 {Black goes ...Bh3, looking to trade off the light-squared bishops. We'll proceed by taking the knight.} 13.Bxf6 13...gxf6 {Black recaptures. Now we advance our d-pawn, taking space and attacking Black's knight.} 14.d5 14...Ne7 {Black moves their knight back.} 15.Qd2 {And we go Qd2, connecting the rooks and eyeing the h6-square. White is doing well. Our plan from here is to bring the queen to h6 and advance g4 in order to carve out a path for our knight to join the attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 6"]
