1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...Be6 {Black's second option is to develop their light-squared bishop to e6. This is played 27% of the time. We'll respond by striking in the center.} 9.d4 9...exd4 {Black captures the d4-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight.} 10.Nxd4 10...Nxd4 {Black decides to trade the knights. And we recapture with our queen. Black has two options in this position but the more common one, castling, directly transposes to the main line.} 11.Qxd4 11...Qd7 {Black's other option is ...Qd7. The point is to follow up with ...Bh3 and trade the light-squared bishops. Against this, we'll go Bg5, putting some pressure on Black's knight.} 12.Bg5 12...Qe7 {The most common and natural option is ...Qe7, defending the knight. We'll respond by pushing our f-pawn.} 13.f4 13...h6 {Black goes ...h6, attacking the bishop.} 14.Bh4 {And we'll slide the bishop back. White is better here because of the space advantage and piece activity. We'll proceed with a3, followed by bringing the a-rook to the d-file. A timely e5-push is also on the cards.} *

