..Qd7 {Black's other option is ...Qd7. The point is to follow up with ...Bh3 and trade the light-squared bishops. Against this, we'll go Bg5, putting some pressure on Black's knight.} 12.Bg5 12...Ng4 {A move like ...Ng4 loses immediately.} 13.Qxg7 {We can simply capture the g7-pawn. After the forced ...Rf8, White is simply up a pawn and Black's king is stuck in the center of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: The Main Line (3...Nf6, 4...Bc5, and 5...d6) Part 1 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/a07ef1e9-4bba-478e-8d99-2f0cfe635bc3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This chapter will cover the move 2...Nc6, which is, just like 2...Nf6, played roughly a third of the time. This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. 3...Nf6 is played 42% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black now has three options, one of which is bishop to c5 which we will study here.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {By far the most popular way to proceed is ...Bc5, developing the bishop to the most logical square. This is played half the time. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. The knight is great on e2 because it supports future d4/f4 advances.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop. This move is played half the time, but has a pretty notable downside. It isolates Black's dark-squared bishop on the queenside and there's a way for us to take advantage of that. We'll go after Black's bishop with Na4, exploiting the fact that Black's bishop can no longer retreat to e7.} 6.Na4 6...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 62% of the time. We'll remove Black's bishop.} 7.Nxb6 7...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. We'll proceed by castling our king.} 8.O-O 8...Bg4 {Black's third option is to develop their bishop and pin our knight. This is played 21% of the time. We'll go f3, attacking the bishop.} 9.f3 9...Be6 {Black's most logical retreat is ...Be6. And now we'll advance our d-pawn, looking to open up the center and threatening to fork Black's pieces.} 10.d4 10...exd4 {Black captures the d4-pawn. We'll recapture with our knight. If Black now plays a slow move, we'll proceed with our plan of fianchettoing the dark-squared bishop.} 11.Nxd4 11...d5 {Black's most active way to proceed is to break in the center with ...d5. We'll respond by taking the bishop.} 12.Nxe6 12...fxe6 {Black recaptures. Now we'll capture the d5-pawn.} 13.exd5 13...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with their queen. Don't rush to trade queens here...} 14.c3 {Instead, we'll play c3, simply controlling the b4- and d4-squares. White has a nice edge because of the bishop pair and Black's structure. We don't mind piece trades here, as long as they don't improve Black's structure. Endgames are favorable for White due to Black's isolated e-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...h5 {Black can also go for ...h5, with the idea to dissuade us from castling. This is rare, but important to cover. Against ...h5, we'll play h3.} 6.h3 6...d6 {60% of the time, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. We'll go for d3, opening up our dark-squared bishop and supporting e4. Our gameplan against ...h5 revolves around playing for f4 and queenside castling. This means that we want to go Bg5, followed by Qd2 and f4.} 7.d3 7...Be6 {Black proceeds by developing their light-squared bishop. Black's plan is to castle queenside. We'll develop our dark-squared bishop and pin Black's knight.} 8.Bg5 8...Qd7 {Black goes ...Qd7, unpinning the knight and preparing ...O-O-O. We play Qd2, improving our queen and preparing queenside castling.} 9.Qd2 9...O-O-O {Black castles queenside. And now we advance our f-pawn! The threat is f5 to trap the bishop.} 10.f4 10...exf4 {Natural for Black is to capture.} 11.gxf4 {And we'll recapture with the g-pawn. White has a winning advantage. f5 and d4 are serious threats to win material and Black is in trouble. O-O-O is also always on the cards.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...O-O {Black can also simply castle, which is played 28% of the time. We'll also proceed by castling.} 6.O-O 6...a6 {Black plays ...a6. The point is to free up a7 for the dark-squared bishop. This is the only unique approach after 5...O-O and is played 12% of the time. I suggest we go d3, supporting e4 and opening up the c1-bishop. Our plan in this position revolves around the f4 advance. This means that we need to unpin the f-pawn.} 7.d3 7...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. We'll respond with h3, preventing any ...Bg4 and freeing up the h2-square. Our plan is now Kh2, followed by f4.} 8.h3 8...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop, preparing to advance ...d5. We play Kh2, unpinning the f-pawn. If Black now plays a slow move like ...Qd7, we can simply advance with f4.} 9.Kh2 9...d5 {Black strikes in the center, which is the most direct approach. We'll respond by advancing f4.} 10.f4 10...dxe4 {By far the more common of the two options is to capture the e4-pawn. This is played 75% of the time. We'll respond by advancing our f-pawn and attacking Black's bishop. Remember this move! The position gets quite tactical from here.} 11.f5 11...exd3 {Black responds by capturing on d3 and counterattacking our knight. We'll proceed by taking the bishop.} 12.fxe6 12...dxe2 {Black captures our knight. We take on f7 with check first. An intermezzo.} 13.exf7+ 13...Rxf7 {Black recaptures with their rook. And now we take on e2 with our queen. Black is up a pawn, but we've got the bishop pair in an open position and Black's king isn't the safest.} 14.Qxe2 14...Nd4 {Natural is also ...Nd4, attacking our queen and eyeing the c2-pawn. We'll respond with Qc4, attacking Black's bishop and pinning the f7-rook.} 15.Qc4 15...Bd6 {Black moves their bishop to safety. Now we make use of the pin along the f-file with Ne4, threatening Ng5.} 16.Ne4 16...b5 {Black responds with ...b5, attacking our queen and looking to force it away from the light-squared diagonal. We'll proceed by taking the knight.} 17.Nxf6+ 17...gxf6 {Black recaptures with the g-pawn. And we slide our queen back to d3. Black's a8-rook is under attack and Bd5 is a threat to win an exchange.} 18.Qd3 18...c6 {Black has to play ...c6 in order to deal with both threats. We'll go c3, attacking Black's knight.} 19.c3 19...Ne6 {Black retreats their knight. We proceed by taking the c6-pawn.} 20.Bxc6 20...Rc8 {Black goes ...Rc8, saving their rook and attacking our bishop.} 21.Bd5 {And we respond by sliding our bishop back to d5, attacking Black's knight. White has a winning advantage. The material is equal, but our pieces are dominant and Black's king isn't safe. We can look to proceed with moves like Qf5 and Bh6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...O-O {Black can also simply castle, which is played 28% of the time. We'll also proceed by castling.} 6.O-O 6...a6 {Black plays ...a6. The point is to free up a7 for the dark-squared bishop. This is the only unique approach after 5...O-O and is played 12% of the time. I suggest we go d3, supporting e4 and opening up the c1-bishop. Our plan in this position revolves around the f4 advance. This means that we need to unpin the f-pawn.} 7.d3 7...h6 {Black's other option is ...h6, covering g5 and giving some luft to the king. This is played 33% of the time. From our point of view, this move is just a waste of a tempo since our plan has nothing to do with the g5-square. We'll respond with h3, freeing up h2.} 8.h3 8...d6 {Black goes ...d6, opening up the light-squared bishop and supporting e5. We play Kh2, unpinning the f-pawn.} 9.Kh2 9...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop, perhaps preparing ...d5. Now we advance our f-pawn!} 10.f4 10...exf4 {Black proceeds by taking the pawn. And we recapture with the g-pawn. Our plan here is simple - advance with f5 and attack Black on the kingside.} 11.gxf4 11...Nd4 {A natural way for Black to proceed is ...Nd4, looking to trade the knights. We'll go Ng3, declining the trade.} 12.Ng3 12...c6 {Black goes ...c6, with the idea to prepare a future d-pawn advance. We go f5, attacking Black's bishop and opening up our c1-bishop.} 13.f5 13...Bd7 {Black moves the bishop to safety, and we develop our bishop. White is better and we've got great attacking prospects on the kingside. We can look to proceed with Qd2.} 14.Be3 14...Nxc2 {...Nxc2 is a tactic that doesn't work. We can simply take Black's bishop.} 15.Bxc5 15...Nxa1 {And when Black takes our rook...} 16.Bxd6 {We capture the d6-pawn. Black's rook is under attack and the knight is completely trapped. White is winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...O-O {Black can also simply castle, which is played 28% of the time. We'll also proceed by castling.} 6.O-O 6...d6 {Played over 60% of the time is ...d6. This, however, quickly transposes. We go after Black's bishop with Na4.} 7.Na4 7...Bb6 {Black moves their bishop back. Now we capture Black's bishop.} 8.Nxb6 8...axb6 {And when Black recaptures...} 9.d4 {We advance our d-pawn, directly transposing into the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...O-O {Black can also simply castle, which is played 28% of the time. We'll also proceed by castling.} 6.O-O 6...a6 {Black plays ...a6. The point is to free up a7 for the dark-squared bishop. This is the only unique approach after 5...O-O and is played 12% of the time. I suggest we go d3, supporting e4 and opening up the c1-bishop. Our plan in this position revolves around the f4 advance. This means that we need to unpin the f-pawn.} 7.d3 7...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. We'll respond with h3, preventing any ...Bg4 and freeing up the h2-square. Our plan is now Kh2, followed by f4.} 8.h3 8...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop, preparing to advance ...d5. We play Kh2, unpinning the f-pawn. If Black now plays a slow move like ...Qd7, we can simply advance with f4.} 9.Kh2 9...d5 {Black strikes in the center, which is the most direct approach. We'll respond by advancing f4.} 10.f4 10...dxe4 {By far the more common of the two options is to capture the e4-pawn. This is played 75% of the time. We'll respond by advancing our f-pawn and attacking Black's bishop. Remember this move! The position gets quite tactical from here.} 11.f5 11...exd3 {Black responds by capturing on d3 and counterattacking our knight. We'll proceed by taking the bishop.} 12.fxe6 12...dxe2 {Black captures our knight. We take on f7 with check first. An intermezzo.} 13.exf7+ 13...Rxf7 {Black recaptures with their rook. And now we take on e2 with our queen. Black is up a pawn, but we've got the bishop pair in an open position and Black's king isn't the safest.} 14.Qxe2 14...h6 {Black plays ...h6, stopping any Bg5 ideas and giving luft to their king. We go Ne4, attacking Black's bishop.} 15.Ne4 15...Nxe4 {Black captures the knight. We respond by capturing the rook.} 16.Rxf7 16...Kxf7 {Black recaptures with their king. And now we capture the knight. Some piece trades have occured, but we've got an active queen and Black's king is wide open. The immediate threat is Qc4+ to fork Black's king and bishop.} 17.Qxe4 17...Qd6 {Black goes ...Qd6, activating their queen and defending their bishop. Now we give a check with our queen.} 18.Qc4+ 18...Ke7 {Black moves their king out of check.} 19.c3 {And we play c3, covering d4 and b4. We're down a pawn, but definitely have sufficent compensation. We'll look to proceed with b4 and Qg4. There's even Ba3 in many positions, looking to advance b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...O-O {Black can also simply castle, which is played 28% of the time. We'll also proceed by castling.} 6.O-O 6...a6 {Black plays ...a6. The point is to free up a7 for the dark-squared bishop. This is the only unique approach after 5...O-O and is played 12% of the time. I suggest we go d3, supporting e4 and opening up the c1-bishop. Our plan in this position revolves around the f4 advance. This means that we need to unpin the f-pawn.} 7.d3 7...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. We'll respond with h3, preventing any ...Bg4 and freeing up the h2-square. Our plan is now Kh2, followed by f4.} 8.h3 8...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop, preparing to advance ...d5. We play Kh2, unpinning the f-pawn. If Black now plays a slow move like ...Qd7, we can simply advance with f4.} 9.Kh2 9...d5 {Black strikes in the center, which is the most direct approach. We'll respond by advancing f4.} 10.f4 10...dxe4 {By far the more common of the two options is to capture the e4-pawn. This is played 75% of the time. We'll respond by advancing our f-pawn and attacking Black's bishop. Remember this move! The position gets quite tactical from here.} 11.f5 11...Bd5 {Black can also respond with ...Bd5. We'll capture Black's bishop.} 12.Nxd5 12...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with their knight. Now we capture on e4 with our bishop.} 13.Bxe4 13...Nf6 {Natural is ...Nf6, targeting our bishop. We'll simply improve our knight.} 14.Nc3 14...Nxe4 {And if Black takes our bishop... We recapture with our knight. Black's bishop is now under attack and f6 is a huge positional threat.} 15.Nxe4 15...Be7 {Black brings their bishop back. We advance with f6, offering up a pawn in order to shred open the Black king.} 16.f6 16...gxf6 {Black takes on f6 with their g-pawn.} 17.Bh6 {And we follow up with Bh6, attacking the rook and creating mating ideas. White is completely winning here.} *

