1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...O-O {Black can also simply castle, which is played 28% of the time. We'll also proceed by castling.} 6.O-O 6...a6 {Black plays ...a6. The point is to free up a7 for the dark-squared bishop. This is the only unique approach after 5...O-O and is played 12% of the time. I suggest we go d3, supporting e4 and opening up the c1-bishop. Our plan in this position revolves around the f4 advance. This means that we need to unpin the f-pawn.} 7.d3 7...d6 {Most often, Black goes ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. We'll respond with h3, preventing any ...Bg4 and freeing up the h2-square. Our plan is now Kh2, followed by f4.} 8.h3 8...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop, preparing to advance ...d5. We play Kh2, unpinning the f-pawn. If Black now plays a slow move like ...Qd7, we can simply advance with f4.} 9.Kh2 9...d5 {Black strikes in the center, which is the most direct approach. We'll respond by advancing f4.} 10.f4 10...exf4 {Black can also go for ...exf4. This is played 17% of the time. We'll recapture with the knight. Black now has to do something about the tension in the center...} 11.Nxf4 11...d4 {Black can also advance the d-pawn. This is rare, but worth investigating.} 12.Nce2 {Against ...d4, we'll go Nce2. White is very slightly better here. Our plan is to target the d4-pawn with c3, and follow up with Qc2 and Bd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: The Main Line Part 2 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/f929714d-e7e4-4d70-945a-cc68848a07c0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {This approach by Black prevents us from advancing in the center, so we need to change our plan... Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop. The general setup we're going for is Bg2, followed by Nge2 and O-O.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {This study will deal with Black's most popular continuation on move three, simply developing their other knight. We'll proceed by fianchettoing.} 4.Bg2 4...Bc5 {Black activates the bishop to c5. And we'll develop our knight to e2 rather than f3.} 5.Nge2 5...h5 {Black can also go for ...h5, with the idea to dissuade us from castling. This is rare, but important to cover. Against ...h5, we'll play h3.} 6.h3 6...h4 {Black can also keep advancing. This is played 23% of the time. Against this, we'll simply push g4. The ability to meet ...h4 with g4 was the main point of our previous move.} 7.g4 7...d6 {Black proceeds with ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. We'll go for d3, opening up our dark-squared bishop and supporting e4.} 8.d3 8...Be6 {Black proceeds by developing their light-squared bishop.} 9.Bg5 {And we'll go Bg5, pinning the knight. Our plan here is to play Qd2, followed by f4 and queenside castling. Nd5 is also a strong idea in many positions.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: Black Plays 4...d6 or 4...Bb4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/b722d746-e992-4a16-912e-19de288eef2d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black goes for 2...Nc6. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {Black activates both knights. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black can now put the bishop on b4.} 4.Bg2 4...Bb4 {Black's second option is ...Bb4. This is played 23% of the time. Against this, we'll simply develop our knight, preparing kingside castling.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Black proceeds with ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. And we go h3, preventing Black from putting a piece on the g4-square.} 6.h3 6...Be6 {Played over 50% of the time is ...Be6. Black develops their bishop and is looking to quickly advance ...d5. We'll respond by jumping our knight into the d5-square, stopping ...d5.} 7.Nd5 7...Bxd5 {Black captures our knight. We recapture.} 8.exd5 8...Ne7 {And Black goes ...Ne7, attacking d5. It seems that we need to defend the d5-pawn, but we can set a trap. We'll simply castle our king. If Black now doesn't take d5, we'll look to proceed by advancing d4.} 9.O-O 9...Nexd5 {And if Black does capture d5... We'll advance our c-pawn, attacking Black's knight and threatening Qa4+. This leads to a forcing sequence...} 10.c4 10...Nb6 {Black has to go ...Nb6 in order to avoid the loss of material. Now we attack Black's bishop.} 11.a3 11...Bc5 {Black has to go ...Bc5 as ...Ba5 would have allowed us to trap the bishop. We advance our b-pawn, attacking Black's bishop once again.} 12.b4 12...Bd4 {Black has to go ...Bd4 as it's the only safe square for the bishop. Now we capture Black's bishop.} 13.Nxd4 13...exd4 {And when Black recaptures... We give a check with our rook. This forces Black to lose castling rights.} 14.Re1+ 14...Kf8 {Black moves their king out of check. We proceed by taking the b7-pawn.} 15.Bxb7 15...Rb8 {Black moves their rook out of danger and attacks our bishop. We bring our bishop back to g2.} 16.Bg2 16...Nxc4 {Black captures the c4-pawn. We go Qc2, attacking Black's knight.} 17.Qc2 17...Ne5 {Black centralizes their knight. Now we develop our dark-squared bishop to b2, attacking the d4-pawn.} 18.Bb2 18...d3 {And when Black goes ...d3, saving the pawn and attacking our queen...} 19.Qa4 {We've got Qa4, attacking a7 and threatening to take Black's knight. At the end of this forced sequence, White is completely winning. We've got the two bishops, more activity, Black's king is bad and Black's queenside is extremely tender.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: Black Plays 4...d6 or 4...Bb4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/b722d746-e992-4a16-912e-19de288eef2d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black goes for 2...Nc6. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {Black activates both knights. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black can now put the bishop on b4.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black can also go ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. This is played 14% of the time. The downside of this move is that it blocks in the dark-squared bishop. We'll proceed by developing our knight to e2 and preparing O-O. Our game plan against 4...d6 is to quickly advance the f-pawn.} 5.Nge2 5...Be7 {Black goes ...Be7, preparing ...O-O. Now we go h3, preventing Black from putting a piece on the g4-square.} 6.h3 6...O-O {Black castles their king. We'll also proceed by castling.} 7.O-O 7...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop, preparing to advance ...d5. Now we advance our f-pawn. A motif we've seen before.} 8.f4 8...exf4 {Black captures the f-pawn. And we recapture with the g-pawn. We've got a nice space advantage. If given the chance, we'll also play d4.} 9.gxf4 9...Bc4 {Black might now go ...Bc4, with the idea being to meet d4 with ...d5. We can respond with d3, attacking the bishop and preventing any trades.} 10.d3 10...Ba6 {And when Black retreats the bishop...} 11.Rb1 {We can go Rb1, preparing b4. White is doing very well here. Black's knight and bishop are very clumsy and our pawns are quickly going to advance up the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: Black Plays 4...d6 or 4...Bb4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/b722d746-e992-4a16-912e-19de288eef2d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black goes for 2...Nc6. Against 2...Nc6, we'll go for g3, preparing to fianchetto our bishop.} 3.g3 3...Nf6 {Black activates both knights. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. Black can now put the bishop on b4.} 4.Bg2 4...Bb4 {Black's second option is ...Bb4. This is played 23% of the time. Against this, we'll simply develop our knight, preparing kingside castling.} 5.Nge2 5...d6 {Black proceeds with ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop and supporting e5. And we go h3, preventing Black from putting a piece on the g4-square.} 6.h3 6...O-O {Black's other option is to castle. This is played 25% of the time. We'll respond by castling as well.} 7.O-O 7...Be6 {Black goes ...Be6, developing their bishop and looking to push ...d5. But we're quicker with the f4-advance!} 8.f4 8...exf4 {Black captures the f-pawn. We recapture with the g-pawn.} 9.gxf4 9...Bc5+ {A natural way for Black to proceed is to give a check with their bishop. We'll advance with d4, blocking the check and attacking Black's bishop.} 10.d4 10...Bb6 {Black moves their bishop to safety. Now we go Kh2, unpinning the d-pawn and threatening a fork.} 11.Kh2 11...d5 {Black prevents us from going d5 by advancing the d-pawn themselves. We should respond by advancing our e-pawn and attacking Black's knight.} 12.e5 12...Ne4 {Black responds with ...Ne4. The idea here is that if we capture twice on e4, our d4-pawn is hanging. So instead of carelessly taking, we'll go ahead and advance our f-pawn.} 13.f5 13...Nxc3 {Black proceeds by trading knights. We recapture with the b-pawn, solidifying the central structure.} 14.bxc3 14...Bd7 {And when Black moves their bishop out of danger...} 15.f6 {We can advance our f-pawn! White is winning here. Black has no defense against the kingside attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...d6 {The other move of interest is ...d6, played 10% of the time. The only unique way for Black to play here is to go for a quick queenside castle. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The most common way for Black to proceed here is ...Nf6, but it just transposes to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...Be6 {Black develops their bishop. The idea is to go ...Qd7 and castle. We'll proceed by developing our knight, preparing to advance d4.} 5.Nge2 5...Qd7 {Black goes ...Qd7, preparing queenside castling. And we strike in the center with d4! Our plan against this particular setup will be to go for queenside castling.} 6.d4 6...exd4 {Most often, Black takes the pawn. This is played 67% of the time. We'll recapture with our knight.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nxd4 {Black decides to trade the knights. And we recapture with our queen. The plan is now Be3 and O-O-O.} 8.Qxd4 8...O-O-O {If Black now carelessly castles...} 9.Qxa7 {We can simply grab the a7-pawn. White is completely winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...Bc5 {Our first move of interest in this study is 3...Bc5, developing the bishop. This is played 30% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The natural ...Nf6 would now transpose to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black goes ...d6, opening up their light-squared bishop. The main idea behind Black's setup is to delay ...Nf6 and advance ...f5. The moment we see ...d6 on the board, we go after Black's bishop. Black has two options here...} 5.Na4 5...f5 {Black can also go ...f5 immediately. This is rare, but worth analyzing. We'll capture Black's bishop.} 6.Nxc5 6...dxc5 {Black recaptures. And we play d3, supporting the e4-pawn and opening up our bishop.} 7.d3 7...fxe4 {Black can also capture the e4-pawn. We'll respond by developing our bishop and attacking the c5-pawn. Remember this tricky idea!} 8.Be3 8...Nf6 {A better way to continue is ...Nf6. This quickly leads to an endgame. We'll respond by taking the e4-pawn.} 9.dxe4 9...Qxd1+ {Black now trades the queens. We recapture with our rook.} 10.Rxd1 10...Nd4 {Logical is ...Nd4, making use of the outpost and attacking the c2-pawn. We go c3, attacking Black's knight.} 11.c3 11...Nc2+ {Black gives a check with their knight. We go Kd2, attacking Black's knight.} 12.Kd2 12...Nxe3 {Black is forced to take the bishop.} 13.Kxe3 {And we recapture with our king in order not to ruin our structure. White has a slight edge here. We'll look to proceed with h3 and Nf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...Bc5 {Our first move of interest in this study is 3...Bc5, developing the bishop. This is played 30% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The natural ...Nf6 would now transpose to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black goes ...d6, opening up their light-squared bishop. The main idea behind Black's setup is to delay ...Nf6 and advance ...f5. The moment we see ...d6 on the board, we go after Black's bishop. Black has two options here...} 5.Na4 5...f5 {Black can also go ...f5 immediately. This is rare, but worth analyzing. We'll capture Black's bishop.} 6.Nxc5 6...dxc5 {Black recaptures. And we play d3, supporting the e4-pawn and opening up our bishop.} 7.d3 7...fxe4 {Black can also capture the e4-pawn. We'll respond by developing our bishop and attacking the c5-pawn. Remember this tricky idea!} 8.Be3 8...exd3 {If Black now keeps capturing... We capture Black's c6-knight.} 9.Bxc6+ 9...bxc6 {And when Black recaptures... We've got the devastating Qh5+!} 10.Qh5+ 10...Kf8 {Black can also move the king. Now we capture the c5-pawn.} 11.Bxc5+ 11...Ne7 {And when Black covers up their king...} 12.O-O-O {We proceed by castling queenside. White is totally crushing here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...Bc5 {Our first move of interest in this study is 3...Bc5, developing the bishop. This is played 30% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The natural ...Nf6 would now transpose to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black goes ...d6, opening up their light-squared bishop. The main idea behind Black's setup is to delay ...Nf6 and advance ...f5. The moment we see ...d6 on the board, we go after Black's bishop. Black has two options here...} 5.Na4 5...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common response. This is played 65% of the time. We'll capture Black's bishop.} 6.Nxb6 6...axb6 {Black recaptures with the a-pawn. And we go Ne2, preparing to castle and looking to quickly advance d4. If Black now doesn't go ...f5, we'll castle, advance d4 and play in the style of the previous study.} 7.Ne2 7...f5 {And if Black does go ...f5, making use of the fact that they delayed activating the knight... We'll respond with d3, supporting the e4-pawn and opening up our bishop.} 8.d3 8...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight.} 9.O-O {And we simply castle our king. White has a very slight edge. We'll advance with f4 and look to open up the position for our bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...Bc5 {Our first move of interest in this study is 3...Bc5, developing the bishop. This is played 30% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The natural ...Nf6 would now transpose to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black goes ...d6, opening up their light-squared bishop. The main idea behind Black's setup is to delay ...Nf6 and advance ...f5. The moment we see ...d6 on the board, we go after Black's bishop. Black has two options here...} 5.Na4 5...f5 {Black can also go ...f5 immediately. This is rare, but worth analyzing. We'll capture Black's bishop.} 6.Nxc5 6...dxc5 {Black recaptures. And we play d3, supporting the e4-pawn and opening up our bishop.} 7.d3 7...fxe4 {Black can also capture the e4-pawn. We'll respond by developing our bishop and attacking the c5-pawn. Remember this tricky idea!} 8.Be3 8...exd3 {If Black now keeps capturing... We capture Black's c6-knight.} 9.Bxc6+ 9...bxc6 {And when Black recaptures... We've got the devastating Qh5+!} 10.Qh5+ 10...g6 {Black can block the check with ...g6.} 11.Qxe5+ {But we simply capture the e5-pawn, forking Black's king and rook on h8. White is completely winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...Bc5 {Our first move of interest in this study is 3...Bc5, developing the bishop. This is played 30% of the time. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The natural ...Nf6 would now transpose to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black goes ...d6, opening up their light-squared bishop. The main idea behind Black's setup is to delay ...Nf6 and advance ...f5. The moment we see ...d6 on the board, we go after Black's bishop. Black has two options here...} 5.Na4 5...f5 {Black can also go ...f5 immediately. This is rare, but worth analyzing. We'll capture Black's bishop.} 6.Nxc5 6...dxc5 {Black recaptures. And we play d3, supporting the e4-pawn and opening up our bishop.} 7.d3 7...Nf6 {Most often, Black goes ...Nf6. This is played over 70% of the time. Now we develop our dark-squared bishop, attacking the c5-pawn.} 8.Be3 8...b6 {Natural is ...b6, supporting c5. The issue with this natural response is that it weakens the long diagonal. We proceed by taking the f5-pawn. This capture opens up a discovered attack towards Black's c6-knight.} 9.exf5 9...Bd7 {Black has to defend the knight.} 10.g4 {And we push g4, supporting f5. We're up a clean pawn and will look to proceed with Nf3, h3 and castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...d6 {The other move of interest is ...d6, played 10% of the time. The only unique way for Black to play here is to go for a quick queenside castle. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The most common way for Black to proceed here is ...Nf6, but it just transposes to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...Be6 {Black develops their bishop. The idea is to go ...Qd7 and castle. We'll proceed by developing our knight, preparing to advance d4.} 5.Nge2 5...Qd7 {Black goes ...Qd7, preparing queenside castling. And we strike in the center with d4! Our plan against this particular setup will be to go for queenside castling.} 6.d4 6...exd4 {Most often, Black takes the pawn. This is played 67% of the time. We'll recapture with our knight.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nxd4 {Black decides to trade the knights. And we recapture with our queen. The plan is now Be3 and O-O-O.} 8.Qxd4 8...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. Before Be3, I suggest we go h3 in order to avoid any ...Ng4 ideas.} 9.h3 9...Be7 {Black develops their dark-squared bishop, preparing kingside castling. We proceed by developing our dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O-O.} 10.Be3 10...O-O {Black castles kingside. And we castle queenside. Our plan in this position is to advance our pawn mass on the kingside.} 11.O-O-O 11...c5 {If Black now gets aggressive... We can slide our queen back. Black's one move pawn attack has left them with a weakness on d6.} 12.Qd3 12...b5 {Further aggression with a move like ...b5 is just very bad and ineffective.} 13.e5 {We can simply advance our e-pawn. Black is losing material here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: Black Plays 3...Bc5 or 3...d6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/0897f49e-d26d-42f5-82e9-ce37307b760a/chapters/5dd9d99b-11bf-4e53-bd30-90aeda44c6cb/studies/cb883221-b4bd-4661-8618-3e9264103b2e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with 1...e5. We go for the Vienna with 2. Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black proceeds with 2...Nc6. We go g3, preparing to fianchetto.} 3.g3 3...d6 {The other move of interest is ...d6, played 10% of the time. The only unique way for Black to play here is to go for a quick queenside castle. We'll proceed by fianchettoing. The most common way for Black to proceed here is ...Nf6, but it just transposes to the previous study.} 4.Bg2 4...Be6 {Black develops their bishop. The idea is to go ...Qd7 and castle. We'll proceed by developing our knight, preparing to advance d4.} 5.Nge2 5...Qd7 {Black goes ...Qd7, preparing queenside castling. And we strike in the center with d4! Our plan against this particular setup will be to go for queenside castling.} 6.d4 6...Bh3 {Black can also offer a bishop trade. This is played 22% of the time. We'll respond by taking the bishop.} 7.Bxh3 7...Qxh3 {And when Black recaptures...} 8.Be3 {We'll simply develop our bishop. White has a nice edge here and our plan is to go for Qd3 and O-O-O.} *